@startuml app_shutdown_sequence
' Revised 2026-07-12: DDC scheduler and helper-process drain ownership
' Revised 2026-10-30: every graceful exit runs the Common host's shutdown core once
!include ../dark-theme.puml
title App shutdown / dispose sequence\nAll graceful paths funnel into TrayAppDotNETAppHost.Shutdown (runs once)

participant "User / tray Exit /\nexit request /\nupdate / restart" as USER
participant "Win32 /\nsession end" as WIN
participant "TrayAppDotNETAppHost\n(Common)" as HOST
participant "App.ShutdownServices\n(Brightness cleanup)" as APP
participant CrashHandler as CRASH
participant "Watcher process" as WATCH
participant "BrightnessFlyout\n+ settings window" as UI
participant ProfileManager as PM
participant DDCRecoveryService as DRS
participant MonitorService as MS
participant DisplayService as DS
participant "Per-monitor\nDDCHelperClient" as DHC
participant NightLightProvider as NLP
participant KnownDisplaysStore as KDS
participant "TrayIconPresenter +\nTrayAppDotNETShellTrayIcon" as TRAY
participant "AppSettings\n(TrayXmlFileStore)" as APS

== Path A: graceful exit (tray Exit, hotkey, exit request, update install, rendering restart, Restart from installed) ==
USER -> HOST : TrayAppDotNETAppLifetime.Exit / Restart\n-> desktop lifetime ShutdownRequested / Exit
HOST -> HOST : Shutdown() (IsShuttingDown guard: runs once)
HOST -> HOST : dispose hotkey service, WatcherMonitor,\nupdate check service
HOST -> APP : ShutdownApp
APP -> APP : DisplayIdentifierService.Hide;\ndispose DisplayEventManager
APP -> DRS : Dispose -> clear recovery-needed flag,\ncancel + bounded-drain fallback worker
APP -> MS  : TryDrainQuickly -> BeginDrainAsync(cap)\nreject new work, drain normal + immediate\nbrightness writes, wait active DDC operations
APP -> NLP : Shutdown (gamma tint released)
APP -> MS  : Dispose -> cancel schedulers
MS  -> DS  : Dispose
DS  -> DHC : Dispose each stable-identity client\nkill child if still running\nwait boundedly for process exit
APP -> PM  : Dispose
APP -> KDS : Dispose (after MonitorService and\nProfileManager, which share it; flushes)
APP -> UI  : close warm flyout slot, tray menu,\nsettings window, flyout
APP -> TRAY : dispose presenter, click toggle,\ntray icon (NIM_DELETE)
HOST -> APS : SaveSettingsOnExit\n(implicit save: only while Autosave is on;\natomic temp+replace)
HOST -> HOST : dispose theme;\nexit request listener last;\nTADNLog.Flush
note right of HOST
  Exit code 0, so the watcher stops too.
  The listener goes last: a new instance taking
  over waits for this exit instead of killing the
  process mid-cleanup.
end note

== Path B: Windows session end (sign-out / shutdown) ==
WIN -> HOST : ShutdownRequested while Windows reports\nthe session as shutting down
HOST -> HOST : _sessionEnding = true; Shutdown() (Path A steps)
HOST -> WATCH : exit code 3 (CrashHandler.SessionEndingExitCode)
WATCH -> WATCH : sees SM_SHUTTINGDOWN as the app exits:\nwait, then relaunch only if the session continues\n(counts toward the rapid-restart cap)
note right of WATCH
  Exit code 3 while Windows is not ending the
  session (the C runtime's abort also exits 3)
  is treated as a crash.
end note

== Path C: unhandled exception on any thread ==
CRASH -> CRASH : AppDomain.UnhandledException:\nlog "FATAL UnhandledException", flush,\nEnvironment.Exit(2)
WATCH -> WATCH : exit code not 0/1 -> crash:\nwait 1 s, restart\n(5 restarts within 30 s -> give up with a message box)

== Path D: non-fatal exceptions ==
note over CRASH
  UI dispatcher exceptions: logged in full
  (DescribeException) and handled.
  Unobserved task exceptions: logged in full and
  marked observed (never fatal).
  Threading.Timer callbacks (NightLightRegistry,
  NightLightSettingsHandler) catch and log their own
  exceptions, so a timer fault never tears down the process.
end note

== Path E: watcher dies ==
WATCH -> HOST : WatcherMonitor sees the watcher's\nprocess handle signal -> Exit (Path A)

== Path F: taskkill / hard kill ==
note over APS
  No graceful path. ProcessExit runs the last-chance
  hook where the runtime allows it (Brightness drains
  queued DDC writes and removes the gamma tint).
  Data files are written atomically (temp + replace),
  so a kill mid-write leaves the previous file intact.
end note

@enduml
